      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:10 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 365 of the Bankruptcy Code lets the trustee decide whether the estate will become a party to an executory contract of the debtor. If so, the trustee assumes the contract and the estate steps in to the debtor's shoes. If not, the trustee rejects the contract. The statute's plain text tells us what that means. Rejection constitutes a breach of such contract immediately before the date of the filing of the petition. The debtor will not fulfill any remaining unperformed obligations under the contract, and the counterparty will have a prepetition claim against the debtor for any resulting damages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But you just said, and I think it's correct, that the debtor would be -- rejection means that the debtor has no obligation to perform future duties under the contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 0.91,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How is that relevant? Meaning, yes, I -- I assume that there's both a contractual obligation and a legal obligation under trademark law, but to the extent that there's a rejection of the contract, the property owner is electing to say, as he -- as it is entitled to say under the law, I reject that obligation vis-a-vis you."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If I could just pick up on a couple of the questions about whether trademarks are different and then say a few words about our rule, why Respondents are wrong, and what the United States' interest is here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "mootness. And if we take the exclusive distribution rights off the table, which was the source of the $4 million of claims that Petitioner referred to in their reply at the petition stage that kept the case from being moot, we're left only with a non-exclusive trademark license that has already expired."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why isn't that enough to have -- at least have an acorn of injury for Article III purposes, the uncertainty created by a declaratory judgment that effectively you can't use it? It may not prohibit you from using it, but it sure may cause you to think twice about doing so. And there might be damages available."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I don't believe that this Court's precedent would allow a claim to be based on that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "INTA",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0002-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Among the amici that the other side have are the INTA and other organizations that have gone to Congress many times to ask Congress to adopt an exception similar to (n) for trademark licensees, and Congress has refused to do so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Could you -- MS. SPINELLI: So, just -- JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: -- answer just one question for me? If you continue using the mark, do the damages that you incur after the filing of the bankruptcy -- are they prepetition debt or postpetition debt? Are you going to get a priority for the damages that accrue after you declare bankruptcy -- after bankruptcy had been declared?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SPINELLI",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SPINELLI: And let me explain why that's exactly what should happen. Prior to bankruptcy -- and this is just like a lease, Justice Breyer -- prior to bankruptcy, the debtor conveyed the licensee an interest in its intellectual property. We don't have to call that a property right. It doesn't matter what we call it. But it's a stick in the bundle of sticks, just the same way that a lease grants the tenant a leasehold interest in the landlord's real property. And McCarthy makes this exact analogy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1657",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a5e28d8175ceb6e8c3bc9bdfb099e1a04d86988cdb21ba48f7c8e7066138d461",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a5e28d8175ce-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: One quick question and then there's your third. But -- but -- and, look, outside of bankruptcy or in general, you lease, the lessor leases a trademark to the lessee. Lessor doesn't keep it up, doesn't quality control. Does that stick, which is now in the hands of the lessee, dissolve, disappear --"
    },
